  "id": "Compound::DB00961 - Gene::6329",
  "verbalization": "Mepivacaine, a local anesthetic, is linked to Scn4a through a series of molecular and pharmacological relationships. The evidence suggests that Mepivacaine, as an amide, is included in the broader class of amides, which includes Ropivacaine and Bupivacaine. These amides bind to Scn4a, a voltage-gated sodium channel subunit, indicating a potential mechanism by which these compounds exert their anesthetic effects. The lowest common ancestors across the paths highlight the shared classification of Mepivacaine and its derivatives as amides, reinforcing the notion that they share a common pharmacological pathway. The evidence also underscores the role of amides in the context of local anesthesia, further supporting the connection between Mepivacaine and Scn4a. The integration of these pathways suggests that the anesthetic effects of Mepivacaine are mediated through its interaction with Scn4a, likely via the modulation of sodium channels, which is a key mechanism in the action of local anesthetics.",
